 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186552877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553453 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186552881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553453 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186552916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553453 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553453 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553453 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553453 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186552873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553453 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9e1b0569-2ef4-5afb-bff6-cf96b04525ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553475 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553475 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553475 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553475 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553475 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553475 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553475 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553475 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186553369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553475 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "de0560d7-2411-5cb7-8285-f3b1b348a142" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553499 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553499 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162549" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553499 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553499 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3b/3b626ea7f1db9e9f7cf92441e3669cbca85a8d469515e5185e87d241440d1bde.png)\n\nWhat is the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$ across the curve $C$ shown above?\n\n*Hint: Start by checking whether $F$ is source-free.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553499 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1a74f654-b8c0-5b05-930c-8a74a2b8b3d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553499 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $F$ is *not* a source-free vector field. \n\nSince $F$ is not source-free, we do not have enough information to determine the flux of $F$ through $C.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553508 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553508 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162628" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553508 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553508 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/84/84cfc63faa3514c7b6151d5ab8f3f8e4e7deaafa8739fa637af2f0ce7453b7fb.png)\n\nThe curve ${C}_{1}$ has endpoints $A(1,1)$ and $B(5,4).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-2xy\\,j$ through ${C}_{1}$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector ${n}_{1},$ as shown above.\n\n*Hint: You're given that $F$ is source-free.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553508 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "27c3df67-079e-5e01-9714-60648afd83ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553508 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2xy)=2x-2x=0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $F$ is source-free, we know the flux of $F$ is independent of the path. In particular,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{2}$ is the straight line from $A(1,1)$ to $B(5,4)$ and  ${n}_{2}$ is its unit normal, shown in the diagram below. \n\n![](images/18/18b1a5bdc11ca3c2b4b728cf8df3a3e1c4b6c2fbbe86549e9ad773367495c533.png)\n\nNotice that both ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $F$ across ${C}_{2},$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector ${n}_{2}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParametrizing ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =i+j+t\\cdot [(5\\,i+4j)-(i+j)] \\\\  & =(4t+1)\\,i+(3t+1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,1].$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={x}^{2}=(4t+1)^{2}=16{t}^{2}+8t+1, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-2xy=-2(4t+1)(3t+1)=-24{t}^{2}-14t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t+1)=4, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+1)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}((16{t}^{2}+8t+1)\\cdot (3)-(-24{t}^{2}-14t-2)\\cdot (4))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\,144{t}^{2}+80t+11\\,)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[48{t}^{3}+40{t}^{2}+11t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(48+40+11) \\\\  & =99.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=99.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553534 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553534 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162555" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553534 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553534 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+2xy\\,j,$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0$\n\nII. There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F$\n\nIII. $F$ is a source-free vector field" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553534 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6f712531-9b84-53ea-bba8-2a30378e29b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553534 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+2xy\\,j$ is a vector field with the simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)={x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2x+2x=4x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Indeed, this follows from statement I.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
